<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhingshak-tseten-dorje"><span class="enc-name">Zhingshak Tseten Dorje</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhingshak Tseten Dorje, also called Karma Tseten, was the founder of the Tsangpa dynasty of Tibet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhizdra-river"><span class="enc-name">Zhizdra (river)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhizdra is a river in Kaluga Oblast, Russia, a left tributary of the Oka about 220 km long.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhizhi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhizhi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhizhi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhizhi was a Xiongnu chanyu of the first century BCE, a brother and rival of Huhanye during the civil war that split the Xiongnu Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhol-pillar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhol-pillar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhol Pillar</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhol Pillar is a stone pillar inscribed in Old Tibetan that stands below the Potala Palace in Lhasa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhongdu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhongdu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhongdu, the Central Capital, was the main capital of the Jurchen Jin dynasty from 1153 to 1215, on the site of south-western Beijing.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhongyong-of-wu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhongyong-of-wu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhongyong of Wu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhongyong was, in Chinese tradition, the second son of the Zhou chieftain Gugong Danfu and a brother of Taibo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-daguan"><span class="enc-name">Zhou Daguan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhou Daguan was a Chinese official of the Yuan dynasty who accompanied an embassy sent by Temür Khan to Angkor in 1296–1297.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dewei"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhou-dewei.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhou Dewei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhou Dewei (died 918) was a leading general of the Shatuo state of Jin in northern China during the transition from the Tang to the Five Dynasties.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhou-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhou dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhou dynasty was the longest-ruling royal dynasty of China, which overthrew the Shang around 1046 BCE and nominally ruled until the State of Qin ended it in 256 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-enlai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhou-enlai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhou Enlai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhou Enlai was the first premier of the People's Republic of China, serving from 1949 until his death in 1976, and its foreign minister until 1958.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-fohai"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhou-fohai.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhou Fohai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhou Fohai (1897–1948) was a Chinese politician who attended the founding congress of the Chinese Communist Party in 1921 before joining the Kuomintang.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-qufei"><span class="enc-name">Zhou Qufei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhou Qufei was a Chinese scholar-official of the Southern Song dynasty who served in Guangxi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhou-wen"><span class="enc-name">Zhou Wen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhou Wen was a general of Chen Sheng's Zhangchu during the Dazexiang uprising against the Qin dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-fan-zhi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhu-fan-zhi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhu Fan Zhi</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zhu Fan Zhi ('Records of Foreign Peoples') is a Chinese work completed around 1225 by Zhao Rugua, a Song dynasty superintendent of maritime trade at Quanzhou.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-lingshi"><span class="enc-name">Zhu Lingshi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhu Lingshi was a general of the Eastern Jin dynasty in the service of Liu Yu, the future founder of the Liu Song dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-wen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhu-wen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhu Wen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhu Wen was a Chinese military leader who first fought as a rebel under Huang Chao and then served the Tang Dynasty before turning against it.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhu-xi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhu Xi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhu Xi was a Chinese philosopher of the Southern Song Dynasty and the leading figure of Neo-Confucianism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-ying"><span class="enc-name">Zhu Ying</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhu Ying was an official of Eastern Wu who travelled with Kang Tai on an embassy sent by Sun Quan to Funan, in mainland South-East Asia, in the mid-3rd century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-yougui"><span class="enc-name">Zhu Yougui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhu Yougui was the second emperor of the Later Liang in the Five Dynasties period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhu-youzhen"><span class="enc-name">Zhu Youzhen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhu Youzhen was the last emperor of the Later Liang dynasty in northern China, a son of its founder Zhu Wen.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhuang-qiao"><span class="enc-name">Zhuang Qiao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhuang Qiao was a general of the state of Chu who, according to the historian Sima Qian, led an army to the Lake Dian region of present-day Yunnan in the 3rd century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhuangzi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhuangzi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhuangzi (Zhuang Zhou) was a Daoist philosopher of the 4th century BCE, a native of the State of Song.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhuanxu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhuanxu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhuanxu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhuanxu is a legendary ruler of ancient China, counted among the Five Emperors and said to be a grandson of the Yellow Emperor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zhuge-liang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zhuge Liang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zhuge Liang was the chancellor of Shu Han in the Three Kingdoms period and chief adviser to Liu Bei, whose alliance with Sun Quan led to victory at Red Cliffs in 208.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zia-ol-din-tabatabaee"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zia-ol-din-tabatabaee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zia ol Din Tabatabaee</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zia ol Din Tabatabaee was an Iranian journalist and politician who, with the Cossack officer Reza Khan, carried out the coup of 21 February 1921 that seized Tehran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ziaur-rahman"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ziaur-rahman.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ziaur Rahman</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ziaur Rahman was a Bangladeshi army officer who announced the declaration of independence on radio in March 1971 and commanded forces in the Liberation War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zibhebhu-kamaphitha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zibhebhu-kamaphitha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zibhebhu kaMaphitha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zibhebhu kaMaphitha was a Zulu chief of the Mandlakazi, a branch of the royal house, who fought in the Anglo-Zulu War of 1879.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zichan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zichan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zichan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zichan, personal name Gongsun Qiao, was chief minister of the State of Zheng from 543 BCE until his death in 522 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zidan-abu-maali"><span class="enc-name">Zidan Abu Maali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Zidan Abu Maali, known as Mulay Zidan, was a son of Ahmad al-Mansur and Saadi sultan of Morocco from 1603 to 1627.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ziebice"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ziebice.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ziębice</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ziębice (German Münsterberg) is a town in Lower Silesia, Poland, south of Wrocław.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ziemomysl-of-inowroclaw"><span class="enc-name">Ziemomysł of Inowrocław</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ziemomysł of Inowrocław (c. 1245–1287) was a Polish prince of the Piast dynasty, a son of Casimir I of Kuyavia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zierikzee"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/zierikzee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Zierikzee</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Zierikzee is a historic town on the island of Schouwen-Duiveland in the Dutch province of Zeeland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ziesar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ziesar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ziesar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ziesar is a small town in Brandenburg, Germany, west of Brandenburg an der Havel.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ziggurat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ziggurat</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A ziggurat is a massive stepped temple tower built in ancient Mesopotamia and Elam, with a shrine on its summit.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ziggurat-of-ur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ziggurat of Ur</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ziggurat of Ur is a great stepped temple tower at the ancient city of Ur in southern Iraq, dedicated to the moon god Nanna.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zij-i-sultani"><span class="enc-name">Zij-i Sultani</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zij-i Sultani is the set of astronomical tables compiled at Ulugh Beg's observatory in Samarkand and completed around 1437.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/zimba"><span class="enc-name">Zimba</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Zimba were warrior bands from the Zambezi region who appear in Portuguese sources of the late 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe"><img class="enc-thumb" 